    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>After a hurricane in Port Arthur, the main dispute is often <strong>cause</strong>: wind (covered by TWIA or a wind policy) vs. flood, storm surge, or rising water (covered only by a flood policy).</li>
<li>Wind-driven rain through a storm-created opening is generally a wind/property claim, not a flood claim.</li>
<li>One storm can mean two or three claims, with separate adjusters, deductibles, and deadlines.</li>
<li>Water lines, upper-wall and ceiling damage, debris patterns, timing, and NWS/NHC data are the evidence that settles most allocation questions.</li>
<li>Some policies contain anti-concurrent causation language that can affect mixed wind/flood losses. Policy wording controls.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>In Southeast Texas, a single hurricane can tear off shingles, blow rain through broken windows, and push water across the floor in the same night. Insurance does not treat those as one loss. The question of wind vs. flood damage decides which policy pays, which deductible applies, and whether any coverage responds at all. For Port Arthur homeowners and business owners, getting that allocation right is often the difference between a fair claim and a frustrating one.</p>

<p>This guide explains how insurers separate wind damage from flood damage, why wind-driven rain is treated differently from rising water, what evidence adjusters rely on, and how to handle separate TWIA and flood adjusters. It is general information, not legal advice, and your policy language always controls.</p>

<h2>Why wind vs. flood damage matters so much in Port Arthur</h2>

<p>Jefferson County is a TWIA first-tier coastal county, according to the Texas Department of Insurance. That means many Port Arthur, Groves, Nederland, Port Neches and Sabine Pass properties carry a stack of policies that each cover a different peril:</p>

<ul>
<li>A <strong>homeowners or commercial property policy</strong> that may exclude wind and hail in the catastrophe area.</li>
<li>A <strong>TWIA policy</strong> covering wind and hail.</li>
<li>An <strong>NFIP or private flood policy</strong> covering flood, including storm surge and rising water.</li>
</ul>

<p>Standard homeowners and commercial policies generally exclude flood, and TWIA does not cover it either. So if wet drywall is called "flood," it falls to the flood policy, if one exists. If it is called "wind" or "wind-driven rain," it falls to the wind policy. When the owner has no flood coverage, the label assigned to the damage can decide whether anything is paid for that part of the loss.</p>

<p>Local geography makes this harder. Port Arthur sits low along Sabine Lake and the Gulf, with neighborhoods behind the city's hurricane flood protection levee and seawall system. That system reduces storm surge risk, but it does not stop rainfall flooding. Hurricane Harvey in 2017 and Tropical Storm Imelda in 2019 both flooded large parts of Jefferson County with rain. Areas outside the levee, such as Sabine Pass and Pleasure Island, face more direct surge exposure, as Hurricane Ike showed in 2008.</p>

<h2>How each type of damage is usually classified</h2>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>What happened</th><th>Usually treated as</th><th>Which policy typically responds</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Wind tears shingles or decking off the roof</td><td>Wind damage</td><td>TWIA or wind-covering property policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain blows in through a broken window or torn roof</td><td>Wind-driven rain through a storm-created opening</td><td>TWIA or property policy (subject to opening requirements)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain enters through an old roof with no new opening</td><td>Often wear and tear or deterioration</td><td>Often excluded or disputed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storm surge pushes Gulf or lake water into the home</td><td>Flood</td><td>NFIP or private flood policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Street drainage overwhelmed, water rises into the house</td><td>Flood (surface water)</td><td>NFIP or private flood policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tree falls in high winds and punctures the roof</td><td>Wind damage</td><td>TWIA or property policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roof damage plus floor-level flooding in the same storm</td><td>Mixed-cause loss</td><td>Both: wind policy for wind portion, flood policy for flood portion</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Wind-driven rain is not flood</h2>

<p>A common misunderstanding after a hurricane is that any water inside the house is "flood." It is not. Wind-driven rain that enters through an opening the storm created, such as missing shingles, peeled-back flashing, a blown-out window, or a damaged soffit, is generally evaluated under the wind or property policy.</p>

<p>The catch is the opening. Many policies only cover interior rain damage if wind or hail first creates an opening in the roof or walls. If the insurer believes the rain came through an aging roof or through gaps that existed before the storm, it may deny that part of the claim as wear and tear. That makes exterior documentation essential: photograph every opening and connect it to the storm date.</p>

<p>Wind-driven rain damage also tends to look different from flood damage. It usually shows up high in the structure, in ceilings, attic insulation, the tops of walls, and around window and door frames on the windward side. Flood damage rises from the floor up, roughly level across rooms.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/flooded-living-room-homeowners-adjuster.webp" alt="Homeowners and an adjuster standing in a flooded living room assessing water damage" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Floor-level water that rose from outside is usually a flood claim. Damage above that line may point to wind or wind-driven rain.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>The evidence adjusters use to separate wind from water</h2>

<p>Insurers and their engineers do not decide cause by guesswork. They look for physical and timeline evidence. You should gather the same evidence, ideally before repairs erase it.</p>

<h3>Water lines</h3>
<p>Rising water leaves a line on walls, cabinets, doors, appliances, and fences. A consistent horizontal line across several rooms and the exterior points to flood. Measure it from the floor and photograph it with a tape measure in view. Then document everything above that line separately.</p>

<h3>Damage above the water line</h3>
<p>Wet ceilings, stained upper walls, soaked attic insulation, and damaged roof decking cannot be explained by water rising from the ground. These areas are strong evidence of wind or wind-driven rain. Photograph them from several angles and note which side of the house they are on.</p>

<h3>Debris and exterior patterns</h3>
<p>Wrack lines of grass, marsh debris, and trash on walls or fences show how high water reached outside. Missing shingles, lifted ridge caps, bent metal roofing, damaged fences laid flat in one direction, and broken windows point to wind. Engineers often compare the direction of damage to the storm's wind field.</p>

<h3>Timing</h3>
<p>When did the ceiling start dripping? When did water first come under the door? In many hurricanes the strongest winds arrive before or during the surge or peak rainfall. A simple written timeline, with times from text messages, photos, or videos, can help separate the two. Phone photo metadata often records the exact time.</p>

<h3>Weather data from NWS and NHC</h3>
<p>The National Hurricane Center publishes Tropical Cyclone Reports with landfall details, wind observations, and surge information. The National Weather Service Lake Charles office, which covers Jefferson County, publishes event summaries and local observations. NOAA's Storm Events Database lists reported wind, flood, and surge events by county. These records can support when and where damaging wind occurred compared with when water rose. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> and <a href="/weather-events/">weather events page</a> are good starting points for your address and date.</p>

<h3>Mitigation records</h3>
<p>Water mitigation companies take moisture readings and often produce moisture maps showing which materials were wet and how high. Those maps can show moisture in the ceiling and upper walls of a room that also had floor-level flooding, helping separate the two causes.</p>

