<h2>Working with separate TWIA and flood adjusters</h2>

<p>When a hurricane hits Port Arthur, you may have two or three adjusters on the same property, each working for a different insurer and each focused on the peril their policy covers. That creates predictable problems:</p>

<ul>
<li>The wind adjuster may attribute interior damage to flood, which TWIA does not cover.</li>
<li>The flood adjuster may attribute damage to wind or rain, which the flood policy does not cover.</li>
<li>Damage in the middle of a wall, such as wet drywall two to four feet up, could be claimed by neither.</li>
<li>Each estimate may use different scopes, prices, and depreciation.</li>
</ul>

<p>A few practical steps help:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Report to every insurer promptly.</strong> TWIA says the deadline to report a claim is one year from the date of damage, with possible extension for good cause, but earlier reporting is almost always better. Flood policies have their own requirements, including strict proof of loss rules. Read your flood policy as soon as a loss happens.</li>
<li><strong>Get copies of each estimate.</strong> Compare what each adjuster included. Gaps and overlaps become obvious when the scopes are side by side.</li>
<li><strong>Ask each adjuster how they allocated the damage.</strong> Request any engineering report the insurer relies on.</li>
<li><strong>Do not sign broad releases</strong> or proof of loss forms you do not understand. Ask questions first.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the evidence.</strong> Save samples of damaged materials when practical and photograph anything removed.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> and <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a> pages describe how a public adjuster can help coordinate both sides. For a step-by-step look at TWIA specifically, see our <a href="/twia-windstorm-claim-guide-port-arthur/">TWIA windstorm claim guide</a>.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/radar-weather-data-review.webp" alt="Weather radar and storm data displayed on a screen during a claim review" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Radar, wind observations, and storm reports can help show when damaging wind arrived compared with rising water.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Anti-concurrent causation language in plain English</h2>

<p>Some property policies include what is often called anti-concurrent causation (ACC) language. The general idea is that if an excluded cause, like flood, contributes to a loss at the same time or in any sequence with a covered cause, like wind, the loss is excluded. The wording often appears near the start of the exclusions section and may say something like "regardless of any other cause or event contributing concurrently or in any sequence to the loss."</p>

<p>Why it matters: in a hurricane, wind and water often combine. An insurer may argue that ACC wording excludes damage that both wind and flood contributed to. Policyholders may argue that specific damage was caused by wind alone and can be separated from the flood damage.</p>

<p>How ACC language applies depends on the exact policy form, the facts, and how Texas courts have interpreted similar wording. That is a legal question. If an insurer relies on ACC language to deny a significant part of your claim, talk to a Texas attorney. What a public adjuster can do is document the damage so the wind-caused portion is clearly separated and supported, which is usually the strongest practical position whether or not ACC language is in play.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><span class="callout-title">Read your policy before the storm</span><p>Look at the exclusions section of your homeowners or commercial policy for flood, surface water, and any "concurrently or in any sequence" wording. Check whether your TWIA and flood policies are current. NFIP coverage generally has a 30-day waiting period, so buying it when a storm is already in the Gulf is usually too late.</p></div>

<h2>A real-world pattern: surge vs. wind along the upper Texas coast</h2>

<p>Hurricane Ike made landfall at Galveston on September 13, 2008 as a Category 2, well down the coast from Port Arthur. Even so, NOAA's Storm Events Database records a 14 to 15 foot storm surge at Sabine Pass, the highest water level ever recorded there. The surge did not top the Port Arthur seawall, but wave action on top of the surge did, and at least 100 homes flooded in Port Arthur. The surge also backed up Hillebrandt and Taylor Bayous and the Neches River, and NOAA records at least 4,000 homes flooded countywide. At the same time, a separate NOAA entry describes Category 1 wind damage across Jefferson County, with trees and power lines down on homes and businesses.</p>

<p>That is exactly the kind of storm where allocation fights happen: the same house in Hillebrandt or Sabine Pass could have a tree through the roof from wind and several feet of surge water inside. Each part belongs to a different policy, and each insurer will look hard at the other cause.</p>

<p>Harvey in 2017 was the opposite pattern for much of Port Arthur. The damage was mostly from extreme rainfall flooding, with NWS Lake Charles reporting more than 64,000 homes flooded in Jefferson County. For owners without flood insurance, that kind of loss may fall outside both the homeowners and TWIA policies, which is why flood coverage deserves serious thought even inside the levee. Read more on our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page.</p>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster can help with mixed-cause claims</h2>

<p>A licensed public adjuster represents the policyholder only, not the insurer. On a wind vs. flood claim, that typically means inspecting the property, documenting damage above and below the water line, pulling weather data for the date of loss, preparing a detailed estimate (often in Xactimate), and presenting a clear allocation to each insurer. Port Arthur Public Adjuster can also review the estimates you have already received to look for scope that fell between the policies.</p>

<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. No adjuster can promise a particular result, and coverage always depends on the policy and the facts. If you are dealing with a <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claim</a> that has been split between insurers, a Port Arthur Public Adjuster review can help you see where you stand.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>

<p>In Port Arthur, wind vs. flood damage is not an academic distinction. It decides which insurer pays and how much. Document the water line, document everything above it, record your timeline, pull the weather data, and report to every insurer that may be involved. The better the evidence separating wind from water, the harder it is for any insurer to push your damage onto someone else's policy.</p>
